 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Overview",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "A linear constant-current, constant-voltage charger for one Li-ion cell with an internal P-channel power MOSFET and thermal regulation. No blocking diode or sense resistor is needed, so the minimum circuit is two external parts. It can deliver 500 mA given a board with good heat spreading, and it can run from a USB port."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Charge cycle",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "A cycle starts when VCC is above the UVLO threshold and a 1 % RPROG is connected from PROG to ground, or when a battery is connected to BAT. Below 2.9 V on BAT the charger trickle-charges at about one tenth of the set current to bring the cell up to a safe level. Above 2.9 V it changes to constant current. When BAT reaches 4.2 V it holds the voltage constant and the current falls; the cycle ends when the current drops to one tenth of the set value."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Charge termination",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "A filtered comparator watches PROG. When PROG stays below 100 mV for longer than tTERM (1.8 ms typical), charging stops, the output current is latched off and the part goes to standby with about 40 μA supply current; any load on BAT is then fed by the cell. The filter time keeps brief load transients on BAT from ending the cycle early. C/10 termination does not operate in trickle charge or in thermal limiting."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Recharge",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "In standby a comparator with a 1.8 ms filter (tRECHARGE) keeps watching BAT. If the cell falls below the 4.1 V recharge threshold, roughly 80 % to 90 % of capacity, a new cycle starts: CHRG pulls low again and STDBY goes high impedance. To restart a cycle manually from standby, remove and reapply the input, or shut down and restart through PROG."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Battery reverse protection",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "A cell connected backwards to BAT puts the part in a fault state: no charge current, both status pins high impedance and both LEDs dark; the reversed cell draws under 0.8 mA. Connecting the cell the right way round starts charging automatically. After a reversed cell is removed the BAT capacitor stays negative, so the LEDs do not show the no-battery state at once; fitting a cell correctly starts a charge, or after the capacitor discharges above 0 V the normal no-battery indication returns. With a reversed cell the supply should be about 5 V and no more than 8 V, since a higher supply puts over 10 V across the chip."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Status outputs",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "CHRG and STDBY are open-drain. CHRG is low while charging and high impedance otherwise; STDBY is low when charging is complete. With no battery connected CHRG pulses; with 10 uF on BAT the flash period is about 0.5 to 2 s. A status output that is not used is tied to ground."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Thermal limit",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "An internal feedback loop cuts the set charge current if the die tries to rise above about 120 ℃. The charge current can therefore be set for typical rather than worst-case ambient temperature, with the part lowering the current itself in the worst case. As thermal feedback lowers the current, the PROG voltage drops in proportion."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Undervoltage lockout",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "The charger stays in shutdown until VCC rises above the undervoltage lockout threshold. Once the UVLO comparator has tripped, the charger does not leave shutdown until VCC is 50 mV above the battery voltage."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Manual shutdown",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Removing RPROG so that PROG floats shuts the charger down at any point in a cycle; battery drain falls below 2 μA and supply current below 50 μA. Reconnecting the resistor starts a new cycle. An N-channel MOSFET in series with RPROG does this under signal control."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Stability",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "With a battery on the output the constant-voltage loop is stable without an external capacitor. With no battery an output capacitor reduces ripple. A large low-ESR ceramic output capacitor wants a 1 Ω resistor in series; a tantalum capacitor needs none. In constant-current mode PROG, not the battery, is in the loop, so capacitance on PROG lowers the largest RPROG that stays stable."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Average charge current",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "When a load such as a switching regulator in a low-current mode hangs beside the cell, the average BAT current matters more than the pulses. A simple RC filter on PROG reads the average, with 10k between PROG and the filter capacitor for stability."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Thermal design",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "The SOT23-6 package is small, so above about 400 mA poor heat removal can let thermal regulation reduce the current. A dissipation resistor in the supply line, chosen for the actual supply voltage, helps; 4.6 V at the VCC pin gives the most current, and the resistor is typically 0.5 to 1 Ω. Heat flows from the die to the lead frame, out through the leads, most of all the ground lead, into the board copper, which acts as the heat sink. Copper on the pins should be as wide as possible and spread out into large areas; vias to inner or back layers help. Other heat sources on the board raise the temperature and lower the available current."
  },
  {
   "topic": "VCC bypass capacitor",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Many capacitor types work for input bypass. Multilayer ceramics can self-resonate with high Q and produce high voltage transients at start-up, for example when the charger is plugged into a live supply; an electrolytic or tantalum capacitor is recommended."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Soft start",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "At the start of each cycle the charge current ramps from zero to full scale over about 20 ms, which limits inrush and transient load on the supply."
  },
  {
   "topic": "CHRG pin with a microprocessor",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "CHRG sinks up to 10 mA to drive an LED. With a pull-up, a microprocessor can read it: while charging an internal N-channel MOSFET pulls the pin low against the 100k pull-up; after termination, or with the input below UVLO, the MOSFET is off and the pin is pulled high."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Input reverse polarity",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "Where VCC needs protection against a reversed supply, a series diode will do if the supply has headroom; where the drop must stay small, a P-channel MOSFET can be used instead."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Bench testing and layout",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "BAT (pin 3) connects straight to the cell's positive terminal; an ammeter in series with BAT is not allowed, but one can go in the VCC line. To guard against spikes and glitches, 1 uF and 10 uF electrolytic capacitors are recommended on VIN and BAT, each with a 0.1 uF ceramic beside it where possible, all placed close to the pins. The dissipation resistor is optional; it is added when the current falls short because of heating, sized for the actual supply, typically 0.5 to 1 Ω with 4.6 V at VCC giving the most current."
  }
 ],
